.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt GREP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm grep
.Nd print lines that match patterns
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl EFGivclLnHhoqswxrR
.Op Fl e Ar pattern
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl m Ar num
.Op Fl A Ar num
.Op Fl B Ar num
.Op Fl C Ar num
.Op Fl Fl color Ns Op = Ns Ar when
.Op Fl Fl include Ns = Ns Ar glob
.Op Fl Fl exclude Ns = Ns Ar glob
.Op Fl Fl exclude-dir Ns = Ns Ar glob
.Op Ar pattern
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility searches for
.Ar pattern
in each
.Ar file
or standard input.
By default,
.Nm
prints each line that matches the pattern.
.Pp
If no
.Ar file
is specified, or if
.Ar file
is
.Ql - ,
.Nm
reads from standard input.
When
.Fl r
or
.Fl R
is specified and no files are given,
.Nm
searches the current directory recursively.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl E , Fl Fl extended-regexp
Interpret
.Ar pattern
as an extended regular expression (ERE).
.It Fl F , Fl Fl fixed-strings
Interpret
.Ar pattern
as a list of fixed strings (not regular expressions),
separated by newlines, any of which is to be matched.
.It Fl G , Fl Fl basic-regexp
Interpret
.Ar pattern
as a basic regular expression (BRE).
This is the default.
.It Fl e Ar pattern , Fl Fl regexp Ns = Ns Ar pattern
Use
.Ar pattern
as the search pattern.
This option can be specified multiple times to search for
any of the patterns.
This is useful to protect patterns beginning with
.Ql - .
.It Fl f Ar file , Fl Fl file Ns = Ns Ar file
Obtain patterns from
.Ar file ,
one per line.
.It Fl i , Fl Fl ignore-case
Perform case-insensitive matching.
By default,
.Nm
is case-sensitive.
.It Fl v , Fl Fl invert-match
Invert the sense of matching, to select non-matching lines.
.It Fl c , Fl Fl count
Suppress normal output; instead print a count of matching
lines for each input file.
.It Fl l , Fl Fl files-with-matches
Suppress normal output; instead print the name of each input
file from which output would normally have been printed.
Scanning stops on the first match.
.It Fl L , Fl Fl files-without-match
Suppress normal output; instead print the name of each input
file from which no output would normally have been printed.
.It Fl n , Fl Fl line-number
Prefix each line of output with the 1-based line number
within its input file.
.It Fl H , Fl Fl with-filename
Print the file name for each match.
This is the default when there is more than one file to search.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl no-filename
Suppress the prefixing of file names on output.
This is the default when there is only one file to search.
.It Fl o , Fl Fl only-matching
Print only the matched (non-empty) parts of a matching line,
with each such part on a separate output line.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet , Fl Fl silent
Quiet mode.
Do not write anything to standard output.
Exit immediately with zero status if any match is found,
even if an error was detected.
.It Fl s , Fl Fl no-messages
Suppress error messages about nonexistent or unreadable files.
.It Fl w , Fl Fl word-regexp
Select only those lines containing matches that form whole words.
The match must be at the beginning or end of a line, or preceded
and followed by a non-word character.
.It Fl x , Fl Fl line-regexp
Select only those matches that exactly match the whole line.
.It Fl r , Fl Fl recursive
Read all files under each directory, recursively.
Symbolic links are not followed.
.It Fl R , Fl Fl dereference-recursive
Read all files under each directory, recursively.
Follow all symbolic links.
.It Fl m Ar num , Fl Fl max-count Ns = Ns Ar num
Stop reading a file after
.Ar num
matching lines.
.It Fl A Ar num , Fl Fl after-context Ns = Ns Ar num
Print
.Ar num
lines of trailing context after matching lines.
Places a separator
.Pq Ql --
between contiguous groups of matches.
.It Fl B Ar num , Fl Fl before-context Ns = Ns Ar num
Print
.Ar num
lines of leading context before matching lines.
Places a separator
.Pq Ql --
between contiguous groups of matches.
.It Fl C Ar num , Fl Fl context Ns = Ns Ar num
Print
.Ar num
lines of output context.
Places a separator
.Pq Ql --
between contiguous groups of matches.
.It Fl Fl color Ns Op = Ns Ar when
Surround matching strings with escape sequences to display them
in color on the terminal.
.Ar when
is
.Ql always ,
.Ql never ,
or
.Ql auto .
The default is
.Ql auto .
.It Fl Fl include Ns = Ns Ar glob
Search only files whose base name matches
.Ar glob .
.It Fl Fl exclude Ns = Ns Ar glob
Skip files whose base name matches
.Ar glob .
.It Fl Fl exclude-dir Ns = Ns Ar glob
Skip directories whose base name matches
.Ar glob
during recursive search.
.It Fl Fl help
Display a brief help message and exit.
.It Fl Fl version
Display version information and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits with one of the following values:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
One or more lines were selected.
.It 1
No lines were selected.
.It 2
An error occurred.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Search for the string
.Dq error
in a log file:
.Pp
.Dl $ grep error /var/log/syslog
.Pp
Search case-insensitively for
.Dq warning
in all C source files:
.Pp
.Dl $ grep -ri warning --include='*.c' src/
.Pp
Count the number of lines containing
.Dq TODO
in all files under the current directory:
.Pp
.Dl $ grep -rc TODO .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr awk 1 ,
.Xr sed 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
.St -p1003.1-2008
specification, with additional GNU extensions for
.Fl r ,
.Fl R ,
.Fl Fl color ,
.Fl Fl include ,
.Fl Fl exclude ,
and
.Fl Fl exclude-dir .
The GNU pattern escape extensions
.Ql \es ,
.Ql \eS ,
.Ql \ew ,
and
.Ql \eW
are supported in both basic and extended regular expressions,
and
.Ql \e|
alternation is supported in basic regular expressions,
on all platforms.
The word-boundary escapes
.Ql \eb ,
.Ql \e< ,
and
.Ql \e>
are not supported.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
